// mgeHost64 — SKY-directional ambient receiver (SH1, tasks/lighting.md).
//
// Ambient on every lit path was one flat colour: `a = gFrameData.ambCol.rgb` (or lodSunAmb on the
// distant paths). An upward-facing roof and a downward-facing arch soffit therefore received exactly
// the same ambient, and the only thing separating them was screen-space GTAO — which measures local
// occlusion and has no idea which way the sky is. So undersides, north faces and overhangs all read
// like roofs.
//
// The sky, meanwhile, is already an analytic function of direction. C2 moved the dome's colour out of
// the mesh and into sky.frag's VColSource == 3 branch, which is literally
//     lerp(fogColNear, skyZenith, saturate(dir.z))
// — a light source we were not sampling. The host projects that same function (plus a sun lobe, so
// ambient arrives warm from the sun's SIDE without touching the direct N.L term) into an SH-L1 basis
// once per frame and publishes it into gShadowParams. Evaluating it here is three dot products.
// Building the SH from the SAME dome function the sky draws is the point: the ambient cannot
// disagree with the sky the player is looking at, and it tracks weather with no reload.
//
// THIS ENHANCES, IT DOES NOT REPLACE. The published coefficients are normalised by their own DC, so
// what comes back is a directional FACTOR whose spherical average is 1. Callers keep their existing
// ambient colour and multiply:
//     a = gFrameData.ambCol.rgb * skyAmbFactor(N, worldPosRel);
// At strength 0 that is bit-identical to today's flat ambient; at 1 it is full directionality at the
// SAME overall brightness. The slider can only redistribute ambient, never dim or brighten the scene
// — which is exactly what makes it a safe A/B, and why the host normalises rather than publishing a
// replacement for ambCol.
//
// Pass the GEOMETRIC normal. opaque/alpha/multimap swap N for the GTAO BENT normal when that toggle
// is on; bending toward the unoccluded direction and then sampling directional sky would count the
// same occlusion twice.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SH2 — and how much of that sky can this point actually SEE (tasks/lighting.md).
//
// The SH above answers "which way is the sky brightest" while assuming the whole hemisphere is
// visible. It has no idea a wall is next to you, that you are in a canyon, or under an arch.
// Screen-space GTAO owns the fine end (contact shadows, a couple of hundred units) and only ever
// sees what is ON SCREEN. The band between GTAO's radius and the sky — buildings, cliffs, canyon
// walls, Red Mountain — was covered by nothing.
//
// gSkyHeight is a top-down max-height map of the world (terrain u statics) in a camera-following
// window, published through gShadowParams.skyAOMap. Marching a few taps outward and taking the
// steepest horizon gives that band directly:
//     vis = cos^2(horizon elevation)
// which is exactly the cosine-weighted fraction of a hemisphere left above a horizon at that
// elevation — so this is the physically-shaped term, not a curve that was tuned to look right.
//
// SEVERAL DIRECTIONS, NOT ONE MAX. Tracking a single max over a spiral makes any one tall occluder
// darken the point from every side, which is wrong in exactly the case this feature exists for
// (one wall of an alley, one canyon face). Independent horizons averaged cost the same taps and a
// few more registers, and a wall on one side then removes its own share of the sky rather than all
// of it.
//
// FIVE of them, an ODD count, and that is the whole reason for the number. Morrowind's world sits on
// an 8192-unit cell grid with its buildings square to it — a 4-fold symmetric world. Any EVEN set of
// azimuths is 2-fold symmetric (every direction has its own opposite in the set), so it shares that
// symmetry and aliases against it: the four walls of a courtyard get sampled by the same two axes
// twice. Five directions at 72 degrees have no opposite pair and no common factor with the grid's
// 4-fold symmetry at all, so no wall orientation is ever privileged.
//
// The taps START at an inner radius (default ~256 u) so this does NOT restate what GTAO already
// multiplied into the same ambient — the two are complementary bands, not stacked copies.
//
// Requires (include AFTER opaque.srt.h): gShadowParams.shAr/shAg/shAb + skyParams + skyAOMap + skyVis,
// and gSkyHeight + gSkyVisScreen + gFrameData.lodEye.

#ifndef SKY_AMB_H
#define SKY_AMB_H

#include "waterplane.h.fsl"   // waterPlaneValid() + kWaterIOR — the underwater Snell window below

// UNDERWATER, the sky is only the SNELL WINDOW (see skyAOVisibility). Depth over which a point
// blends from the dome to the window, so the waterline carries no seam — waterplane.h.fsl's one
// surface-optics blend.
#define SKY_SNELL_BLEND kWaterSurfaceBlend

// The five azimuths, 72 degrees apart, phase-shifted 9 degrees off the axes:
//     9, 81, 153, 225, 297 degrees.
// 9 is not arbitrary. Five directions spaced 72 land on five DISTINCT offsets mod 90 (0,18,36,54,72
// shifted by the phase), so they cover a 72-degree spread inside the grid's 90-degree period and
// leave an 18-degree gap. Centring that gap on the axes — phase 9 — is what maximises the smallest
// angle any direction makes with a wall, at 9 degrees. Any other phase puts a tap closer to, or
// straight down, an axis-aligned wall.
//
// Written inline at the point of use rather than in a constant array: the project's other tap loops
// (msmrecv's PCSS disc) build their offsets the same way, and it keeps the samples as independent
// scalars instead of dynamically-indexed vector lanes, which is what lets them coissue.

// Sky visibility 0..1 at an ABSOLUTE world position. 1 = open sky.
// worldAbs.z is this point's own height, which is what every horizon is measured against.
float skyAOVisibility(float3 worldAbs)
{
    float4 m = gShadowParams.skyAOMap;   // xy = map origin (world), z = 1/extent, w = tap count

    // Map-space uv. Outside the window there is no data, and a clamp sampler would smear the edge
    // texel across the whole world beyond it — a wall at the boundary would shadow everything
    // behind it forever. Fade the term out over the outer eighth instead, so distant land leaves
    // the effect smoothly rather than crossing a ring.
    float2 uv = (worldAbs.xy - m.xy) * m.z;
    float2 e  = abs(uv - 0.5f) * 2.0f;
    float  edge = saturate((1.0f - max(e.x, e.y)) * 8.0f);
    if (edge <= 0.0f) { return 1.0f; }

    float inner = gShadowParams.skyParams.z;
    float outer = gShadowParams.skyParams.w;
    int   steps = (int)max(m.w, 1.0f);

    // Geometric step spacing: the near taps decide almost all of the answer (a horizon at 300 units
    // subtends far more sky than one at 6000), so spend samples where the signal is. logRatio is
    // the same for every direction and every step, so it is hoisted out of the whole march.
    float logRatio = log2(max(outer / max(inner, 1.0f), 1.0f));
    float myH      = worldAbs.z;

    // --- THE OVERHANG CORRECTION, and the one place this technique is provably lying ---------------
    //
    // A height map is 2.5D: one number per column, the top. The horizon test below then declares
    // everything BELOW that elevation blocked, which quietly assumes each column is SOLID from -inf up
    // to its stored height. There is no way to encode "the space under that roof is open", and that
    // assumption fails hardest in exactly the places players stand: under Baar Dau over Vivec, on the
    // shaft of a tower with a wider top, under a bridge, an arch, or a canton walkway. The blocker is
    // near and tall, so every one of the five azimuths reports a steep horizon, the five-way average
    // that normally rescues one-sided occluders has nothing one-sided to work with, and the point goes
    // near-black — while the truth is that the sky toward the horizon is open in every direction and
    // the blocker covers only a cone overhead.
    //
    // ONE extra tap detects it: the receiver's OWN column. If there is geometry above my head at my
    // own XY, then this column is demonstrably NOT solid below its stored height — I am standing in
    // the air that the march is about to pretend is rock. That is direct evidence, not a heuristic
    // about shape, and it is the only such evidence the map contains.
    //
    // POINT sampled, deliberately, where every other tap here is bilinear. The question is about MY
    // texel, and bilinear would blend a neighbour's answer into it: stand on open ground a few tens of
    // units from a tower and a bilinear tap returns a large fraction of the tower's height, firing the
    // correction where nothing is overhead at all. Nearest keeps the test honest at the cost of a
    // 64-unit hard edge, which is far below what this low-frequency term resolves anyway.
    //
    // An empty texel carries SKY_HEIGHT_NONE (-30000), so `above` goes hugely negative and the
    // saturate below pins trust at 1 — the sentinel disarms this the same way it disarms the march.
    //
    // Where the map is lying, the answer is to hand the job back to GTAO rather than to invent one:
    // GTAO is screen-space, sees the real geometry, and is already multiplied into this same ambient.
    // The trust FLOOR is what keeps a genuinely huge overhead blocker (the meteor really does take a
    // bite out of the sky) from going to zero occlusion, so this can only ever lighten toward the
    // truth, never past it.
    float selfH = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerPointClamp, uv, 0).r;
    float above = selfH - myH;
    float trust = 1.0f - (1.0f - gShadowParams.skyAO2.z)
                       * saturate((above - gShadowParams.skyAO2.x) / max(gShadowParams.skyAO2.y, 1.0f));

    // One running horizon SLOPE per direction — sin(elevation), which is what the visibility term
    // squares. Four ride a float4 so those marches vectorise into the same instructions; the fifth
    // is a scalar alongside. An odd count cannot pack evenly into vector lanes, and that is a fair
    // trade for not sharing the world's symmetry (see the header note).
    float4 maxS  = f4(0.0f);
    float  maxS4 = 0.0f;

    for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k)
    {
        float t = ((float)k + 1.0f) / (float)steps;
        float d = inner * exp2(t * logRatio);
        // The step is taken in MAP space, so the whole march is five adds off one scaled radius.
        float r = d * m.z;
        float2 o0 = float2( 0.98769f,  0.15643f) * r;   //   9 degrees
        float2 o1 = float2( 0.15643f,  0.98769f) * r;   //  81
        float2 o2 = float2(-0.89101f,  0.45399f) * r;   // 153
        float2 o3 = float2(-0.70711f, -0.70711f) * r;   // 225
        float2 o4 = float2( 0.45399f, -0.89101f) * r;   // 297

        // SampleLvl, not Sample: this header is included by statics.vert, and a vertex stage has no
        // derivatives to pick a mip from. The map has one mip anyway.
        float4 h;
        h.x = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o0, 0).r;
        h.y = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o1, 0).r;
        h.z = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o2, 0).r;
        h.w = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o3, 0).r;
        float h4 = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv + o4, 0).r;

        // sin(elevation) = dh / sqrt(dh^2 + d^2). An empty texel carries SKY_HEIGHT_NONE, so dh is
        // hugely negative there and the max() below discards it with no branch — the sentinel
        // disarms itself.
        float4 dh  = h - f4(myH);
        float  dh4 = h4 - myH;
        maxS  = max(maxS,  dh * rsqrt(dh * dh + f4(d * d)));
        maxS4 = max(maxS4, dh4 * rsqrt(dh4 * dh4 + d * d));
    }

    float4 s   = saturate(maxS);
    float  s4  = saturate(maxS4);
    float4 vis = f4(1.0f) - s * s;          // cos^2(horizon) per direction
    float  vis4 = 1.0f - s4 * s4;

    // UNDERWATER: only the Snell window carries sky. A submerged point sees the sky through the cone
    // within asin(1/n) = 48.6 deg of vertical — elevation >= 41.4 deg — and a horizon below that cone
    // blocks nothing that was reaching it. The cosine-weighted share of the dome above elevation e is
    // cos^2(e), so the window is cos^2(41.4 deg) = 1/n^2 of it, and the part of the window left above
    // a horizon h is cos^2(max(h, 41.4 deg)) / (1/n^2) = min(1, cos^2(h) * n^2). Exact, per wedge,
    // nothing tuned. Blended in over SKY_SNELL_BLEND of depth; exactly the dome above the surface.
    // skyvis.comp.fsl applies the same window to the direction maps.
    if (waterPlaneValid())
    {
        float under = saturate((gShadowParams.waterFogPlane.x - myH) / SKY_SNELL_BLEND);
        if (under > 0.0f)
        {
            const float n2 = kWaterIOR * kWaterIOR;
            vis  = lerp(vis,  min(vis * n2, f4(1.0f)), under);
            vis4 = lerp(vis4, min(vis4 * n2, 1.0f),    under);
        }
    }
    float  ao  = (dot(vis, f4(1.0f)) + vis4) * 0.2f;   // average the five hemispherical wedges

    // Overhang correction (see the note above the self tap): fold the march's answer back toward open
    // by however much this column proved the solid-below assumption wrong. Applied HERE, after the
    // five wedges are averaged, because the lie is a property of the COLUMN and not of any one
    // azimuth — under an overhang every direction is wrong by the same reasoning.
    ao = lerp(1.0f, ao, trust);

    // strength lerps the whole thing back toward "fully open", so 0 is exactly the SH1 image.
    return lerp(1.0f, ao, gShadowParams.skyParams.y * edge);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SKY-VISIBILITY MAPS — the Twister method (tasks/forge-skyao-oracle.md "TWISTER").
//
// The march above reads a 2.5D map, and a 2.5D map cannot say "the space under that root is open"
// or "this tube is closed": it over-lights tunnels and blotches underhangs, and no knob fixes either
// (the oracle fit proved it). The host keeps K depth maps of the static world instead, one per fixed
// sky direction, and skyvis.comp averages them ONCE PER HALF-RES PIXEL after the depth prepass into
// gSkyVisScreen = (vis, distance, coverage). Oracle floor error 0.034 vs the march's 0.129.
//
// This side reads it: the four half-res texels around the fragment, bilinear, each kept only where
// ITS distance matches this fragment's — so a fragment that was not in the prepass (alpha, water,
// another camera) finds no match and takes the march, rather than borrowing what is behind it.
// Bound only into the MAIN PerFrame set; elsewhere it reads 0, whose distance matches nothing.
float skyAOTerm(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    return (gShadowParams.skyParams.y > 0.0f) ? skyAOVisibility(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz)
                                              : 1.0f;
}

// svPos = SV_Position.xy of a MAIN-camera fragment (pixel centres, render rect at 0,0).
float skyAOTermPx(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
    float strength = gShadowParams.skyParams.y;
    if (strength <= 0.0f) { return 1.0f; }
    if (gShadowParams.skyVis.x <= 0.0f) { return skyAOTerm(N, worldPosRel); }

    float  myD = length(worldPosRel);
    float  tol = myD * gShadowParams.skyVis.y + gShadowParams.skyVis.z;
    // Half-res texel h stands for full pixel 2h, whose centre is 2h + 0.5: a fragment centred at
    // x + 0.5 sits at half coordinate x / 2.
    float2 hc = (svPos - 0.5f) * 0.5f;
    int2   i0 = int2(floor(hc));
    float2 fr = hc - float2(i0);
    int2   mx = int2(GetDimensions(gSkyVisScreen, NO_SAMPLER)) - int2(1, 1);
    float  wSum = 0.0f, vSum = 0.0f, cSum = 0.0f;
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k)
    {
        int2   o  = int2(k & 1, k >> 1);
        float4 s  = LoadTex2D(gSkyVisScreen, NO_SAMPLER, clamp(i0 + o, int2(0, 0), mx), 0);
        float  wb = ((o.x == 1) ? fr.x : 1.0f - fr.x) * ((o.y == 1) ? fr.y : 1.0f - fr.y) + 1.0e-3f;
        float  w  = (abs(s.y - myD) <= tol) ? wb : 0.0f;
        wSum += w; vSum += w * s.x; cSum += w * s.z;
    }
    if (wSum <= 0.0f)
    {
        // NO MATCH: a SILHOUETTE, usually — a robe's hem, a head, a root thinner than a half-res
        // texel, whose four neighbours all hold the background. Falling straight to the march lit
        // those pixels as OPEN SKY inside a tunnel (the march cannot see a tunnel), which read as a
        // rim of bright specks on every edge, against any background. Instead take the texel NEAREST
        // in distance over a wider 4x4, if it is loosely on this surface's depth; only a pixel with
        // nothing near it (a root against the open sky, an alpha surface) takes the march.
        float bestE = 1.0e30f;
        float4 best = f4(0.0f);
        for (int by = -1; by <= 2; ++by)
        {
            for (int bx = -1; bx <= 2; ++bx)
            {
                float4 s = LoadTex2D(gSkyVisScreen, NO_SAMPLER, clamp(i0 + int2(bx, by), int2(0, 0), mx), 0);
                float  e = abs(s.y - myD);
                if (s.y >= 0.0f && e < bestE) { bestE = e; best = s; }
            }
        }
        if (bestE > myD * 0.25f + 64.0f) { return skyAOTerm(N, worldPosRel); }
        wSum = 1.0f; vSum = best.x; cSum = best.z;
    }
    float vis = vSum / wSum, cov = cSum / wSum;
    float v = lerp(1.0f, vis, strength);
    if (cov >= 0.999f) { return v; }
    return lerp(skyAOVisibility(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz), v, cov);
}

#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
// THE WORLD PROBE (pgi.comp.fsl, the pool's reserved LAST slot): one probe in open air a few hundred
// units above the camera, captured and relit like the others. Its irradiance takes the sky SH's place
// in the old model below — in ABSOLUTE units (E/pi over ambCol, the probes' own), so the far field and
// every fallback carry the energy and tint the near probes measure ("for open field ... a single probe
// affecting the whole world so they just match", user 2026-10-13). pgiParams3.w = its share (0 = off or
// not built yet: the sky SH, as before). Never inside the relight: a probe's sky stand-in reading the
// world probe would be the world probe reading itself.
float3 pgiWorldFactor(float3 N, float3 fSky)
{
    const float s = gShadowParams.pgiParams3.w;
    if (s <= 0.0f) { return fSky; }
    const float  G  = gShadowParams.pgiParams0.w;
    const float  c  = (G - 0.5f) / G;   // the slot's texel centre: column (G-1, G-1), layer 1
    const float3 uv = float3(c, c, 0.0f);
    const float4 m  = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 15.0f), 0.0f);
    if (m.y < 1.0e-3f) { return fSky; }
    float4 q[7];
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 7; ++k) {
        q[k] = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 7.0f + (float)k), 0.0f) / m.y;
    }
    const float3 n = normalize(N);
    const float Y[9] = { 0.282095f, 0.488603f * n.y, 0.488603f * n.z, 0.488603f * n.x,
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.y, 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.z, 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    const float f[28] = { q[0].x, q[0].y, q[0].z, q[0].w, q[1].x, q[1].y, q[1].z, q[1].w,
                          q[2].x, q[2].y, q[2].z, q[2].w, q[3].x, q[3].y, q[3].z, q[3].w,
                          q[4].x, q[4].y, q[4].z, q[4].w, q[5].x, q[5].y, q[5].z, q[5].w,
                          q[6].x, q[6].y, q[6].z, q[6].w };
    float3 E = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) { E += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Y[b]; }
    const float3 fw = max(E, f3(0.0f)) * gShadowParams.pgiParams0.x / max(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb, f3(1.0e-4f));
    return lerp(fSky, fw, s * saturate(m.y));
}
#endif

// worldPosRel = the CAMERA-RELATIVE world position every one of the six lit paths already carries
// (near geometry is pre-shifted by -eye, distant statics are host-shifted, terrain subtracts
// lodEye in its VS). gFrameData.lodEye is the absolute eye and is written EVERY frame, interiors
// included, so this reconstruction is the same one water.frag uses for its world-anchored normals.
float3 skyAmbFactorAO(float3 N, float ao)
{
    // `ao` = the SH2 occlusion, computed by the caller (skyAOTerm / skyAOTermPx), on its OWN strength
    // lane so the two halves A/B independently — SH off with AO
    // on is a legitimate thing to want to look at, and it would be unreachable if AO hung off the
    // SH's early-out. At AO strength 0 not one tap is taken and this whole block folds away, which
    // is the zero-change proof the feature ships behind. Interiors publish 0 in BOTH lanes (there
    // is no sky to project and no map to march), so neither needs a shader branch on interior-ness.

    // SH strength 0 = the directional half is off. AO still applies — it is a separate measurement.
    // THE FLOOR (shadowparams.h.fsl skyAOFloor): what the occluded share keeps — a typical interior's
    // ambient, as a fraction of THIS frame's ambient, capped. Added as fl * (1 - ao), so open sky
    // (ao = 1) is bit-for-bit what it was and a sealed tunnel (ao = 0) gets exactly the floor.
    float ambL = dot(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb, float3(0.299f, 0.587f, 0.114f));
    float fl   = (ambL > 1.0e-6f) ? min(gShadowParams.skyAOFloor.x / ambL, gShadowParams.skyAOFloor.y) : 0.0f;
    float fill = fl * (1.0f - ao);

    float s = gShadowParams.skyParams.x;
    float3 fs = f3(1.0f);
    if (s > 0.0f)
    {
        float4 n = float4(normalize(N), 1.0f);
        float3 f = float3(dot(gShadowParams.shAr, n),
                          dot(gShadowParams.shAg, n),
                          dot(gShadowParams.shAb, n));
        // An L1 fit of a strongly directional field goes negative on the far side. Clamp rather than
        // rescale: rescaling would drag the whole sphere back toward flat, which is the one thing this
        // is for. The clamp only ever adds a hair of energy, so the "cannot darken the scene" property
        // survives it.
        // The AO scales the whole factor. SH1's "can only redistribute, never dim" guarantee is a
        // property of the SH ALONE and is deliberately not extended here — removing light is precisely
        // what an occlusion term is for. Keeping them as separate multiplies is what lets the panel
        // A/B either one against the other.
        fs = lerp(float3(1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f), max(f, float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f)), s);
    }
    // The WORLD PROBE, where it is built, in the sky SH's place (pgiWorldFactor above).
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    fs = pgiWorldFactor(N, fs);
#endif
    return fs * ao + f3(fill);
}


// GI (tasks/forge-gi.md v2): the occluded share's fill from the GI FIELD — what the surroundings
// actually reflect (sunlit sand, shadowed rock, water) — instead of the constant floor. The field is
// low-frequency RGB radiance in the same units the lit surfaces carry, so dividing by ambCol turns it
// into this function's factor. Falls back to the floor wherever the field has no data (alpha 0:
// unbound in this set, outside the window, not yet settled) so a missing field reads as today.
// LATERAL OPENNESS 0..1: the share of five azimuths along which this point looks out SIDEWAYS through
// free air. The sky AO cannot tell a giant mushroom's underside (cap overhead, the lit field all around)
// from a tunnel's deep end (cover overhead AND on every side): both see almost no sky. Sideways they
// differ, and the two top-down maps together hold it: gSkyHeight is each column's TOP, gSkyHeightLow
// minus its lowest STATIC surface. A column is free air at my height if I am above its top, or below
// its lowest static (the air under a cap; terrain never folds into the low map). A direction is open
// only if every step out to R is free, so a root wall a few hundred units thick closes it however
// open the field beyond (user, 2026-10-11: "a mushroom in the open field, pretty dark underbelly"
// while "tunnel is dark, opening is lit ... we want to keep this").
float skyLateralFree(float2 uv, float z)
{
    // BILINEAR: point taps drew the 64 u texels as squares (user, 2026-10-11). At a static's edge the
    // low map blends toward its none-sentinel, which reads as open air — a soft edge, on the open side.
    float top = SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeight,    gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv, 0).r;
    float low = -SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyHeightLow, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv, 0).r;   // none -> +30000
    return max(saturate((z - top) * (1.0f / 32.0f)), saturate((low + 32.0f - z) * (1.0f / 32.0f)));
}

float skyLateralOpen(float3 worldAbs, float R)
{
    float4 m  = gShadowParams.skyAOMap;
    float2 uv = (worldAbs.xy - m.xy) * m.z;
    float  r  = R * m.z;
    float  sum = 0.0f;
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 5; ++k)
    {
        float  az = 0.15708f + 1.25664f * (float)k;   // 9 degrees + k * 72
        float2 d  = float2(cos(az), sin(az)) * r;
        float  f  = skyLateralFree(uv + d * 0.3333f, worldAbs.z);
        f = min(f, skyLateralFree(uv + d * 0.6667f, worldAbs.z));
        f = min(f, skyLateralFree(uv + d,           worldAbs.z));
        sum += f;
    }
    return sum * 0.2f;
}

// PROBE CAPTURE back-face test (statics/terrain frag mode 5, pgigather): the viewer is behind a
// surface where dot(cross(ddx P, ddy P), N) * PGI_BACK_SIGN > 0. The sign is the capture pass's
// screen handedness, set by measurement (wrong = every probe rejected, the field all old model).
#define PGI_BACK_SIGN (-1.0f)
#define PGI_BACK_SENTINEL 3.0f
// The PROBE CAPTURE's G-buffer mode: mode 5 (albedo) PLUS the back-face flag. Its own number because the
// flag's sign is the capture pass's handedness: in the main view it fired on every front face and the
// F12 albedo view (5) showed magenta everywhere (user, 2026-10-15). Set only on the capture's
// FrameData copy (host g_capFace.dbg); never in kDebugModeNames.
#define PGI_CAPTURE_DBG 64u   // a captured texel whose red exceeds this was a back face (4, 0, 4)
// ...and the probe GI's own variant of it (knob pgiEmis, tasks/forge-gi.md P2): a CHECKERBOARD, even
// pixels the albedo, odd pixels the EMITTED radiance (the lit part that is not base texture — emissive,
// glow layers). The target has no lane to spare and a second pass would double the capture; the gather
// averages the two parities apart. The probe oracle keeps 64: its faces are read texel by texel.
#define PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS 65u
// TEST (pgiEmis 2): the same capture with EVERY distant static emitting its texture (statics.vert emissive
// 1) — the emission path end to end on a scene with no glow of its own.
#define PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_TEST 66u
// The PROBE ORACLE's emission view (probeOracleModes 5, I2): EVERY pixel the emitted radiance — its faces
// are read texel by texel, so no checkerboard. The independent truth adds it as a source (a lava room's
// light is its lava); litTerm - ambient cancels emission, so without this lane the truth had none.
#define PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_ALL 67u
#define PGI_IS_EMIS(d) ((d) == PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS || (d) == PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_TEST || (d) == PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_ALL)
bool pgiCapEmisPx(float2 pos) { return ((((uint)pos.x) ^ ((uint)pos.y)) & 1u) != 0u; }
// The emission pixels of capture mode d: the odd checkerboard, or all of them under PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_ALL.
bool pgiCapEmisPxD(uint d, float2 pos) { return d == PGI_CAPTURE_EMIS_ALL || pgiCapEmisPx(pos); }
// THE CAPTURE'S ALBEDO IS THE EFFECTIVE DIFFUSE REFLECTANCE: texture x the material tint MW's routing
// multiplies the light by (vColSource 2: the vertex colour; else MatDiffuse). The relight multiplies its
// light by the captured albedo, and with the texture alone it missed every interior's baked vertex-colour
// darkening: stored probes 1.5x the independent truth in the census office (2026-10-20).
float3 pgiCapTint(uint vColSource, float3 vcol, float3 matDiffuse) { return (vColSource == 2u) ? vcol : matDiffuse; }
// PHYSICAL ALBEDO (gShadowParams.calParams.z = exponent g, 0 = off; host knob albedoPhys; user 2026-10-20).
// MW's textures decode to 0.05-0.2 linear — darker than any real plaster or wood — and a bounce returns
// about the albedo, so the probes measured shadows fed by 3% bounce and the exposure, metering the lamp
// pools, crushed them. The texture's LUMINANCE goes to L^g (chroma kept), capped at a physical 0.9:
// sRGB 0.7 plaster 0.45 -> 0.62, sRGB 0.4 wood 0.13 -> 0.29 at g 0.6. Texture albedo only — never a
// dark map, a vertex tint or an emitter. Frame and probe captures alike (same frags).
// TOE: L^g's gain L^(g-1) is unbounded toward black — a near-black texel (L 1e-4, a doorway's painted
// shadow, all its little energy green) came out 40x, a vivid green patch (census office, user 2026-10-21).
// Under PHYS_ALBEDO_TOE the gain fades to 1 (lerp to L by L / toe; monotone for any toe < 1): painted
// black stays black, real dark materials (0.05+) keep the whole lift.
#define PHYS_ALBEDO_TOE 0.04f
float3 physAlbedo(float3 a)
{
    const float g = gShadowParams.calParams.z;
    if (g <= 0.0f) { return a; }
    const float L = dot(a, float3(0.2126f, 0.7152f, 0.0722f));
    if (L <= 1.0e-6f) { return a; }
    const float w = saturate(L / PHYS_ALBEDO_TOE);
    float3 o = a * lerp(1.0f, pow(L, g) / L, w);
    const float mx = max(max(o.r, o.g), o.b);
    return (mx > 0.9f) ? o * (0.9f / mx) : o;
}
// VERTEX-COLOUR SPLIT (debugParams.w bit 7, host knob vcolSplit; user 2026-10-20). MW paints vertex colours
// for HUE and for baked AO-like DARKNESS, and as an albedo multiplier the darkness made the albedo too dark —
// dark bounces, then a sky blown out by the exposure lifting them. Split against the mesh's own unoccluded
// level `ref` (client: 95th percentile of each vertex's brightest channel, in matAmbient.x on DiffAmb
// draws; 0 = none): with m the vertex's brightest channel, the DIRECT light (and the probes' albedo) take
// vcol + max(0, ref - m) — the missing brightness given back as a NEUTRAL lift — and the AMBIENT keeps
// vcol: the darkness dims the indirect light only. A lift, not a scale (vcol x ref / m): the scale
// amplified the meaningless hue of a near-black vertex to full brightness (a green doorway in the census
// office, 2026-10-20). Returns the lift; 0 = no split.
float vcolSplitLift(uint vColSource, float3 vcol, float ref, uint flags)
{
    if (vColSource != 2u || (flags & 128u) == 0u || ref <= 1.0e-3f) { return 0.0f; }
    return max(0.0f, ref - max(max(vcol.r, vcol.g), vcol.b));
}
// ...so the PROBE ORACLE's lighting views (its litTerm and ambient jobs) are the UNTINTED d + a and a:
// the truth is albedo x (litTerm - ambient), and the tint is in its albedo now. F12 6 and 7 are unchanged.
// Frags without a material tint (statics, terrain, grass) show their 6 / 7 for these.
#define PGI_ORACLE_LIGHT 68u
#define PGI_ORACLE_AMB   69u
// 70: the RELIGHT's direct light at this pixel (pgipoint.h.fsl, the probes' own formula: the sun unshadowed as
// indoors, the lanterns with their atlas shadows, no gobo) — the oracle's full-resolution relight (probeOracleModes 6).
#define PGI_ORACLE_RELIGHT 70u
uint pgiOracleAsView(uint d) { return (d == PGI_ORACLE_LIGHT || d == PGI_ORACLE_RELIGHT) ? 6u : d == PGI_ORACLE_AMB ? 7u : d; }

#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
// PROBE GI (pgi.srt.h): E/pi at N from the probe field — the same units ambCol x factor has — and the
// field's confidence (a). Columns bilinear (WRAP: the grid is toroidal), the two layers of a column
// blended by height between their probes. Every stored value is premultiplied by validity, so a
// half-built neighbourhood divides out exactly. a = 0 outside the window or with no probe built.
#define PgiSampleArr(T, uv, s) T.SampleLevel(gSamplerBilinearWrap, float3(uv, (float)(s)), 0.0f)
float4 pgiIrradiance(float3 N, float3 worldAbs, out float below)
{
    below = 0.0f;
    const float4 P0 = gShadowParams.pgiParams0;
    const float4 P1 = gShadowParams.pgiParams1;
    if (P0.x <= 0.0f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    const float2 e = abs(worldAbs.xy - P1.xy) * P1.z;
    const float edge = saturate((1.0f - max(e.x, e.y)) * 6.0f);
    if (edge <= 0.0f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    const float2 uv = worldAbs.xy * P0.y + P0.z;
    const float4 m0 = PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, 14);
    const float4 m1 = PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, 15);
    float w0 = 0.0f, w1 = 0.0f;
    // BELOW THE LOWEST PROBE: a probe stands on its cell's lowest FLOOR (pgiplace), so a point well
    // under its column's layer 0 has that probe ABOVE it — on top of a cover spanning the cell, as a
    // tunnel's roof (whose rock reaches the ground, so no floor is found beneath it). The caller then
    // gates the field by the point's openness. Under a floating cover (a giant mushroom's cap) layer 0
    // stands on the terrain beneath it, so the underside is ABOVE its probe and takes the field.
    // A layer-0 probe found INSIDE geometry drops out (pgirelight); where none is left around this
    // point — a tunnel through a hill, whose ground-level probes sit in the hill — the height that
    // says "the probe is above you" is layer 1's.
    if (m0.y > 1.0e-3f)      { below = saturate((m0.x / m0.y - worldAbs.z - 64.0f) * (1.0f / 48.0f)); }
    else if (m1.y > 1.0e-3f) { below = saturate((m1.x / m1.y - worldAbs.z - 64.0f) * (1.0f / 48.0f)); }
    if (m0.y > 1.0e-3f && m1.y > 1.0e-3f) {
        const float z0 = m0.x / m0.y, z1 = m1.x / m1.y;
        const float t = saturate((worldAbs.z - z0) / max(z1 - z0, 1.0f));
        w0 = 1.0f - t; w1 = t;
    } else if (m0.y > 1.0e-3f) { w0 = 1.0f; }
    else if (m1.y > 1.0e-3f)   { w1 = 1.0f; }
    // SLAB VISIBILITY: a probe lights only the heights it can see — between the FLOOR and CEILING it
    // measured straight down and up (pgirelight). A point past them is across a slab from the probe:
    // a tunnel's floor over a hollow with a probe in it ("tunnel ... lits up", the yellow wall), the
    // hill over the tunnel with layer 1 on top, a cap's top over its own underside. Invisible layers
    // drop out and the visible one takes the whole weight; none visible = the old model here.
    const float zr = worldAbs.z;
    float v0 = 0.0f, v1 = 0.0f;
    // Slack 256 u + a 128 u fade past the measured surface: a dome's height (the receivers on a
    // cap's underside sit on it, above its centre hit) but not a hill's rock (the tunnel ~600 u up).
    if (m0.y > 1.0e-3f) {
        v0 = saturate((m0.z / m0.y + 384.0f - zr) * (1.0f / 128.0f)) * saturate((zr - m0.w / m0.y + 384.0f) * (1.0f / 128.0f));
    }
    if (m1.y > 1.0e-3f) {
        v1 = saturate((m1.z / m1.y + 384.0f - zr) * (1.0f / 128.0f)) * saturate((zr - m1.w / m1.y + 384.0f) * (1.0f / 128.0f));
    }
    {
        const float a0 = w0 * v0, a1 = w1 * v1, sa = a0 + a1;
        if (sa > 1.0e-4f) { w0 = a0 / sa; w1 = a1 / sa; }
        else if (v0 > 0.0f || v1 > 0.0f) { w0 = (v0 >= v1) ? 1.0f : 0.0f; w1 = 1.0f - w0; }   // the visible one
        else { return f4(0.0f); }
    }
    const float vis = max(v0, v1);
    const float wsum = w0 * m0.y + w1 * m1.y;
    if (wsum < 1.0e-3f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    // CONFIDENCE against the probes that CAN light this point: each layer's bilinear validity over
    // the share of its corners not REJECTED (inside geometry, pgirelight lane 27). A probe inside a
    // castle wall is no reason to fall back to the old model beside it — its neighbours cover it.
    const float rj0 = PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, 6).w, rj1 = PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, 13).w;
    const float conf = w0 * m0.y / max(1.0f - rj0, 1.0e-3f) + w1 * m1.y / max(1.0f - rj1, 1.0e-3f);
    const float inv = 1.0f / wsum;
    float4 c[7];
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 7; ++k) {
        c[k] = (w0 * PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, k) + w1 * PgiSampleArr(gPgiSH, uv, 7 + k)) * inv;
    }
    // Flattened coefficients q = 3 * basis + channel, four per plane.
    const float3 n = normalize(N);
    const float Y[9] = { 0.282095f, 0.488603f * n.y, 0.488603f * n.z, 0.488603f * n.x,
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.y, 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.z, 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    const float f[28] = { c[0].x, c[0].y, c[0].z, c[0].w, c[1].x, c[1].y, c[1].z, c[1].w,
                          c[2].x, c[2].y, c[2].z, c[2].w, c[3].x, c[3].y, c[3].z, c[3].w,
                          c[4].x, c[4].y, c[4].z, c[4].w, c[5].x, c[5].y, c[5].z, c[5].w,
                          c[6].x, c[6].y, c[6].z, c[6].w };
    float3 E = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) {
        E += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Y[b];
    }
    return float4(max(E, f3(0.0f)), edge * saturate(conf) * vis);
}

// PROBE GI VOLUME (ball placement; pgi.comp.fsl PGI_SPLAT): E/pi at N and the volume's confidence.
// Trilinear: xy by the wrap sampler (the volume is toroidal), z by hand between two slices of each
// plane. Premultiplied by validity, divided out here; a = validity x the window-edge fade.
// lan = the lanterns' L0 here (E/pi, pgi.comp.fsl pgiPointLight): the sky gate leaves up to that much alone.
// bw = per-BAND weights on the stored (cosine-convolved) SH: (1,1,1) is irradiance, which every diffuse
// receiver wants; pgiSpecBands hands the specular lookup its own lobe instead (skyAmbSpecPx).
// eIrr = the same coefficients as plain irradiance (bw = 1): smooth and positive, the normalization pgiHiSpec
// needs (a separate loop, so E's own arithmetic is untouched and callers that drop it compile it away).
float4 pgiVolIrradianceW(float3 N, float3 worldAbs, out float vis, out float3 lan, float3 bw, out float3 eIrr)
{
    vis = 1.0f;
    lan = f3(0.0f);
    eIrr = f3(0.0f);
    const float4 P2 = gShadowParams.pgiParams2;
    const float4 P3 = gShadowParams.pgiParams3;
    const float VX = P2.y, VZ = P2.z;
    // NORMAL BIAS (DDGI's): looked up half a voxel out along the normal. A voxel whose centre is inside
    // the surface's own solid sees no probe (the splat's visibility test cuts it), and a lookup AT the
    // surface leaned on it — thick roots and rocks lost their confidence and showed the old model.
    // VIEW BIAS (DDGI's surface bias = normal + view; knob pgiViewBias): the offset leans toward the
    // CAMERA, whose ray to a visible point crosses only open air. Along N alone, a root's edge facing a
    // thin tunnel wall looked up half a voxel INTO the wall — the outside's voxel, lit by the outside's
    // probes (the Telvanni root, user 2026-10-15). P2.w = 1 + bias + 4 x round(8 x view share).
    const float wq = P2.w - 1.0f;
    const float vk = floor(wq * 0.25f);
    const float3 Vd = normalize(gFrameData.lodEye.xyz - worldAbs);
    // An UP-facing surface keeps N: straight up from a floor is open air, and a grazing view ray there
    // barely lifts the lookup off the ground (open floors lost 12% to the old-model blend). Walls,
    // edges and ceilings take the view share whole.
    const float3 nn = normalize(N);
    float3 bd = lerp(nn, Vd, vk * 0.125f * saturate(1.0f - nn.z));
    bd = (dot(bd, bd) > 1.0e-6f) ? normalize(bd) : Vd;
    const float3 c = (worldAbs + bd * ((wq - 4.0f * vk) / P2.x)) * P2.x;   // continuous voxel coordinate (centre = i + 0.5)
    const float3 r = c - P3.xyz;                           // within the window: [0, dims)
    const float3 e = min(r, float3(VX, VX, VZ) - r);       // distance to the window's faces (voxels)
    const float edge = saturate(min(min(e.x, e.y), e.z * 2.0f) - 1.0f);
    if (edge <= 0.0f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    const float2 uv = c.xy / VX;
    const float cz = c.z - 0.5f;
    const float z0 = floor(cz), t = cz - z0;
    const float s0 = z0 - VZ * floor(z0 / VZ), s1 = (z0 + 1.0f) - VZ * floor((z0 + 1.0f) / VZ);
    const float4 m = lerp(PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, 7.0f * VZ + s0), PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, 7.0f * VZ + s1), t);
    if (m.x < 1.0e-3f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    const float inv = 1.0f / m.x;
    vis = saturate(m.y * inv);   // the probes' sky visibility here (pgi.comp.fsl slice 16 + layer)
    lan = lerp(PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, 8.0f * VZ + s0), PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, 8.0f * VZ + s1), t).rgb * inv;   // ...and their lanterns' L0
    float4 q[7];
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 7; ++k) {
        q[k] = lerp(PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, (float)k * VZ + s0), PgiSampleArr(gPgiVol, uv, (float)k * VZ + s1), t) * inv;
    }
    const float3 n = normalize(N);
    const float Y[9] = { bw.x * 0.282095f, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.y, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.z, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.x,
                         bw.z * 1.092548f * n.x * n.y, bw.z * 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, bw.z * 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                         bw.z * 1.092548f * n.x * n.z, bw.z * 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    const float f[28] = { q[0].x, q[0].y, q[0].z, q[0].w, q[1].x, q[1].y, q[1].z, q[1].w,
                          q[2].x, q[2].y, q[2].z, q[2].w, q[3].x, q[3].y, q[3].z, q[3].w,
                          q[4].x, q[4].y, q[4].z, q[4].w, q[5].x, q[5].y, q[5].z, q[5].w,
                          q[6].x, q[6].y, q[6].z, q[6].w };
    float3 E = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) { E += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Y[b]; }
    const float Yi[9] = { 0.282095f, 0.488603f * n.y, 0.488603f * n.z, 0.488603f * n.x,
                          1.092548f * n.x * n.y, 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                          1.092548f * n.x * n.z, 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    float3 Ei = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) { Ei += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Yi[b]; }
    eIrr = max(Ei, f3(0.0f));
    return float4(max(E, f3(0.0f)), saturate(m.x) * edge);
}
float4 pgiVolIrradiance(float3 N, float3 worldAbs, out float vis, out float3 lan)
{
    float3 eIrr;
    return pgiVolIrradianceW(N, worldAbs, vis, lan, f3(1.0f), eIrr);
}

// THE SPECULAR LOBE ON THE PROBES' SH (knob pgiSpecSharp, gShadowParams.pgiSpec.x).
//
// The volume stores IRRADIANCE: radiance convolved with the cosine lobe, i.e. band 1 x 2/3, band 2 x 1/4
// (E/pi units). Looked up in the MIRROR direction that is the environment as a roughness-1 surface sees
// it, whatever the surface — a polished sphere reflected the field's mean with three quarters of its
// band-2 contrast gone, which reads GREY (user 2026-10-17, "shiny spheres ... looks gray from SH").
// These weights undo the cosine and apply this surface's lobe instead: GGX at alpha = rough^2 as a
// Phong lobe of exponent s = 2/alpha^2 - 2, whose SH attenuation is exp(-l^2 / 2s) (Ramamoorthi &
// Hanrahan), floored at the cosine's own value so roughness 1 is irradiance EXACTLY. Band 0 is never
// touched: the mean — the energy — stays the field's; only direction and tint come back.
// ⚠ NINE COEFFICIENTS HOLD NOTHING SHARPER THAN AN L2 LOBE: below roughness ~0.5 every surface looks
// about equally sharp (the mirror's weights 1.5 / 4 are reached by then). A mirror IMAGE is a different
// source (the reflection pass, or prefiltered probe cubes). Ringing past a bright sky is clamped at 0
// in the evaluation; k blends the deconvolution in (0 = irradiance, the shipped look).
float3 pgiSpecBands(float rough, float k)
{
    const float a2 = max(rough * rough * rough * rough, 1.0e-4f);
    const float s  = max(2.0f / a2 - 2.0f, 1.0e-3f);
    const float b1 = max(exp(-0.5f / s), 2.0f / 3.0f) * 1.5f;
    const float b2 = max(exp(-2.0f / s), 0.25f) * 4.0f;
    return float3(1.0f, lerp(1.0f, b1, k), lerp(1.0f, b2, k));
}

// HI-RES WORLD PROBE (knob pgiHiRes; pgi.comp.fsl PGI_CUBE). The world probe's SH through band weights bw
// (its slot: column (G-1, G-1), layer 1 — pgiWorldFactor's fetch), E/pi; valid = its validity.
float3 pgiWorldE(float3 N, float3 bw, out float valid)
{
    const float  G  = gShadowParams.pgiParams0.w;
    const float  c  = (G - 0.5f) / G;
    const float3 uv = float3(c, c, 0.0f);
    const float4 m  = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 15.0f), 0.0f);
    valid = m.y;
    if (m.y < 1.0e-3f) { return f3(0.0f); }
    float4 q[7];
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 7; ++k) {
        q[k] = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 7.0f + (float)k), 0.0f) / m.y;
    }
    const float3 n = normalize(N);
    const float Y[9] = { bw.x * 0.282095f, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.y, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.z, bw.y * 0.488603f * n.x,
                         bw.z * 1.092548f * n.x * n.y, bw.z * 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, bw.z * 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                         bw.z * 1.092548f * n.x * n.z, bw.z * 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    const float f[28] = { q[0].x, q[0].y, q[0].z, q[0].w, q[1].x, q[1].y, q[1].z, q[1].w,
                          q[2].x, q[2].y, q[2].z, q[2].w, q[3].x, q[3].y, q[3].z, q[3].w,
                          q[4].x, q[4].y, q[4].z, q[4].w, q[5].x, q[5].y, q[5].z, q[5].w,
                          q[6].x, q[6].y, q[6].z, q[6].w };
    float3 E = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) { E += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Y[b]; }
    return max(E, f3(0.0f));
}

// A smooth surface's environment from the world probe's CUBE — the detail nine coefficients cannot hold —
// with the volume's ENERGY AND TINT: the cube at R is scaled per channel by (the volume's irradiance here) /
// (the world probe's own irradiance), both at R. Open ground near the world probe: ratio ~1, the cube as
// captured. A tunnel: the volume is dark, so is its reflection — the world probe's bright outside never
// leaks in. Roughness past the cut (pgiSpec.z) is the SH lobe alone; the cube's mips span 0..cut.
// ⚠ THE RATIO IS TAKEN ON IRRADIANCE (eIrr), NOT ON THE SHARPENED LOBE. The cube already carries the
// detail; the ratio only has to carry energy and tint, and the sharpened lobe rings toward zero past a
// bright horizon — per channel that drew a RAINBOW band along the reflected horizon (2026-10-17: gone
// with pgiSpecSharp 0, i.e. with smooth SH on both sides). Irradiance is smooth and positive.
// pgiSpec.y = strength (published 0 until the cube is built and resting readable), w = its last mip.
#define PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP 4.0f   // forgerender.cpp kPgiCubeMips - 1

// Any probe slot's own irradiance at N (pgiWorldE's lookup at that slot's texel; layer = slot / G^2).
float3 pgiSlotE(float slotF, float3 N, out float valid)
{
    const float  G    = gShadowParams.pgiParams0.w;
    const uint   g    = (uint)G;
    const uint   slot = (uint)max(slotF, 0.0f);
    const uint   layer = slot / (g * g), s2 = slot % (g * g);
    const float3 uv   = float3(((float)(s2 % g) + 0.5f) / G, ((float)(s2 / g) + 0.5f) / G, 0.0f);
    const float4 m    = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 14.0f + (float)layer), 0.0f);
    valid = m.y;
    if (m.y < 1.0e-3f) { return f3(0.0f); }
    float4 q[7];
    UNROLL for (int k = 0; k < 7; ++k) {
        q[k] = gPgiSH.SampleLevel(gSamplerPointClamp, uv + float3(0.0f, 0.0f, (float)layer * 7.0f + (float)k), 0.0f) / m.y;
    }
    const float3 n = normalize(N);
    const float Y[9] = { 0.282095f, 0.488603f * n.y, 0.488603f * n.z, 0.488603f * n.x,
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.y, 1.092548f * n.y * n.z, 0.315392f * (3.0f * n.z * n.z - 1.0f),
                         1.092548f * n.x * n.z, 0.546274f * (n.x * n.x - n.y * n.y) };
    const float f[28] = { q[0].x, q[0].y, q[0].z, q[0].w, q[1].x, q[1].y, q[1].z, q[1].w,
                          q[2].x, q[2].y, q[2].z, q[2].w, q[3].x, q[3].y, q[3].z, q[3].w,
                          q[4].x, q[4].y, q[4].z, q[4].w, q[5].x, q[5].y, q[5].z, q[5].w,
                          q[6].x, q[6].y, q[6].z, q[6].w };
    float3 E = f3(0.0f);
    UNROLL for (int b = 0; b < 9; ++b) { E += float3(f[3 * b + 0], f[3 * b + 1], f[3 * b + 2]) * Y[b]; }
    return max(E, f3(0.0f));
}

// The direction to look a near cube up with, for a ray from P along R: the SPHERE PROXY (user, 2026-10-18),
// its radius taken from the cube itself — alpha is the mean INVERSE hit distance in that direction (pgi.comp.fsl
// PGI_CUBE near mode; 0 = sky, i.e. no parallax). A first guess from the coarsest mip at R, then once more at
// the direction that guess hit. d = P - C. A receiver outside its sphere (c > 0 with the ray leaving) or a hole
// (alpha < 0) falls back to R.
float3 pgiNearDir(float3 d, float3 R, uint which)
{
    float3 dir = R;
    UNROLL for (int it = 0; it < 2; ++it) {
        const float  lodR = (it == 0) ? PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP : PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP - 2.0f;
        const float  inv  = (which == 0u) ? SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube0, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lodR).a
                                          : SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube1, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lodR).a;
        if (inv < 0.0f) { return dir; }
        const float  r  = 1.0f / max(inv, 1.0e-5f);
        const float  b  = dot(d, R);
        const float  c  = dot(d, d) - r * r;
        const float  disc = b * b - c;
        if (c > 0.0f || disc < 0.0f) { return dir; }
        dir = normalize(d + R * (-b + sqrt(disc)));
    }
    return dir;
}

// How much a near probe COVERS P (0 = none): full out to 75% of its reach, then a short fade. NOT a long ramp,
// and no roughness term (the caller's outer lerp already trades the cube for the SH by roughness): a partial
// weight shows the world cube through it, the same scene from another viewpoint — a building with a roof and
// a faded second roof (user, 2026-10-19). Cheap — no fetch. Returns -1 for a probe that is not live.
float pgiNearCover(float4 nP, float3 P, out float dist)
{
    dist = 1.0e30f;
    if (nP.w < 0.0f || gShadowParams.pgiNearP.x <= 0.0f) { return -1.0f; }
    const float reach = max(gShadowParams.pgiNearP.y, 1.0f);
    dist = length(P - nP.xyz);
    return gShadowParams.pgiNearP.x * (1.0f - smoothstep(0.75f * reach, reach, dist));
}

// One near cube's radiance for a receiver at P (absolute), normalized like the world cube by THIS probe's own
// irradiance. Returns rgb and the weight it earns (w = pgiNearW's, 0 = none survives the lookups).
float4 pgiNearOne(float4 nP, uint which, float3 P, float3 R, float rough, float cut, float3 eIrr, float w)
{
    const float3 d = P - nP.xyz;
    float valid;
    const float3 Ep = pgiSlotE(nP.w, R, valid);
    if (valid < 1.0e-3f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    const float3 dir = pgiNearDir(d, normalize(R), which);
    const float  lod = saturate(rough / cut) * PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP;
    float4 L0 = (which == 0u) ? SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube0, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lod)
                              : SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube1, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lod);
    if (L0.a < 0.0f) {   // a hole (a back face): two mips up, as the world cube fills one
        L0 = (which == 0u) ? SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube0, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lod + 2.0f)
                           : SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiNearCube1, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, dir, lod + 2.0f);
        if (L0.a < 0.0f) { return f4(0.0f); }
    }
    const float3 ratio = lerp(f3(1.0f), min(eIrr / max(Ep, f3(1.0e-4f)), f3(8.0f)), saturate(gShadowParams.pgiSpec.w));
    return float4(L0.rgb * ratio, w * saturate(valid));
}

// P = the receiver's ABSOLUTE position (the near probes' parallax and reach). The near probes' cubes win where
// the receiver is near them; the world cube takes what they leave.
float3 pgiHiSpec(float3 R, float rough, float3 E, float3 eIrr, float3 P)
{
    const float4 H = gShadowParams.pgiSpec;
    if (H.y <= 0.0f) { return E; }
    const float cut = max(H.z, 0.05f);
    const float w = H.y * saturate(1.0f - rough / cut);
    if (w <= 0.0f) { return E; }
    float valid;
    const float3 Ew = pgiWorldE(R, f3(1.0f), valid);
    if (valid < 1.0e-3f) { return E; }
    // a = the texel's coverage (a back face is a hole, pgi.comp.fsl PGI_CUBE): filled from two mips up.
    const float  lod = saturate(rough / cut) * PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP;
    const float4 L0  = SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiWorldCube, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, R, lod);
    const float3 L   = (L0.a >= 0.999f) ? L0.rgb
                     : lerp(SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiWorldCube, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, R, lod + 2.0f).rgb, L0.rgb, saturate(L0.a));
    // H.w = how much of the normalization applies (knob pgiHiNorm; 0 = the cube's radiance as captured — the
    // truth test for a mirror: does it match the scene beside it?).
    const float3 ratio = lerp(f3(1.0f), min(eIrr / max(Ew, f3(1.0e-4f)), f3(8.0f)), saturate(H.w));
    float3 hi = L * ratio;
    // H2: the near probes over the world cube. Each probe's COVERAGE (pgiNearCover) is near-binary, and the two
    // split P by a SHARP distance share (power 8: a narrow band at their midpoint mixes them, everywhere else one
    // owns it) — any wide mix of two viewpoints draws every edge twice. The weaker is looked up only inside that
    // band, so most receivers pay for one lookup.
    float d0, d1;
    const float c0 = pgiNearCover(gShadowParams.pgiNear0, P, d0);
    const float c1 = pgiNearCover(gShadowParams.pgiNear1, P, d1);
    if (c0 > 0.0f || c1 > 0.0f) {
        float s0 = (c0 > 0.0f) ? 1.0f : 0.0f;   // probe 0's share of the near term
        if (c0 > 0.0f && c1 > 0.0f) {
            const float r = d0 / max(d1, 1.0f);
            const float r2 = r * r, r4 = r2 * r2;
            s0 = 1.0f / (1.0f + r4 * r4);
        }
        // Explicit branches, not ?: — HLSL's ?: may evaluate both arms, which is the whole cost.
        float4 n0 = f4(0.0f), n1 = f4(0.0f);
        if (s0 >= 0.5f) {
            n0 = pgiNearOne(gShadowParams.pgiNear0, 0u, P, R, rough, cut, eIrr, s0 * c0);
            if (s0 < 0.9f) { n1 = pgiNearOne(gShadowParams.pgiNear1, 1u, P, R, rough, cut, eIrr, (1.0f - s0) * max(c1, 0.0f)); }
        } else {
            n1 = pgiNearOne(gShadowParams.pgiNear1, 1u, P, R, rough, cut, eIrr, (1.0f - s0) * c1);
            if (s0 > 0.1f) { n0 = pgiNearOne(gShadowParams.pgiNear0, 0u, P, R, rough, cut, eIrr, s0 * max(c0, 0.0f)); }
        }
        const float ws = n0.a + n1.a;
        if (ws > 0.0f) { hi = lerp(hi, (n0.rgb * n0.a + n1.rgb * n1.a) / ws, saturate(ws)); }
    }
    return lerp(E, hi, w * saturate(valid));
}

// ONE LEVEL OF RECURSION in a probe capture (user, 2026-10-20: "we can have one level of recursive, use global
// probe, so spheres don't look white"). A capture writes ALBEDO, so a chrome sphere was relit as a white diffuse
// ball. What a PBR surface REFLECTS there comes from the WORLD probe's cube, in the env term's factor units (the
// caller multiplies by its ambient colour and the env BRDF), and rides the emission pixels; 0 = no cube yet. The
// cube itself is never read through this in the pass that builds it, so this is one level and no more.
float3 pgiCapSpec(float3 R, float rough)
{
    if (gShadowParams.pgiSpec.y <= 0.0f) { return f3(0.0f); }
    const float lod = saturate(rough / max(gShadowParams.pgiSpec.z, 0.05f)) * PGI_CUBE_LASTMIP;
    return max(SampleLvlTexCube(gPgiWorldCube, gSamplerTrilinearClamp, R, lod).rgb, f3(0.0f));
}
#endif

// The pre-probe ambient factor (sky SH x sky AO + the GI field / floor). skyAmbFactorAOp below wraps it.
float3 skyAmbFactorAOpOld(float3 N, float ao, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    // OPENNESS, from the POSITION's own sky visibility (before the ground-facing exemption below). The
    // field is a TOP-DOWN map of what the outside reflects; a spot reaches that light only through the
    // same openings it sees sky through. A tunnel's deep end sees neither, so it keeps the constant
    // floor (dark, as before GI); its entrance sees both and takes the bounce (user, 2026-10-11: "the
    // tunnel should stay dark, additional GI should work on the entrance only").
    const float openRef = gShadowParams.skyAOGround.y;
    // SMOOTHSTEP, not a linear ramp: a short ramp with a hard knee drew the knee as a line across a
    // wall ("below 0.2 it also has a boundary", user 2026-10-11).
    float       open    = (openRef > 0.0f) ? smoothstep(0.0f, openRef, ao) : 1.0f;
    const float aoPos   = ao;
    // ...or open SIDEWAYS (skyLateralOpen): only asked where the sky test said closed and the normal has
    // a ground-facing share for it to matter to, so open ground pays nothing.
    const float latR = gShadowParams.skyAOGround.w;
    if (open < 1.0f && latR > 0.0f && normalize(N).z < 0.9f && gShadowParams.skyAOMap.z > 0.0f)
    {
        open = max(open, skyLateralOpen(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz, latR));
    }
    // The sky AO is a POSITION measurement (is the sky above this point blocked?), so it may only
    // occlude the share of the normal that LOOKS at the sky. The ground-facing share (0.5 - 0.5 N.z of
    // the cosine lobe) sees the ground, which a cap or a roof above does not block: a mushroom's
    // underside was losing its whole ambient to a cover it faces away from. GTAO still darkens contact.
    // Gated by OPENNESS: the ground a sealed spot faces is the enclosure's own dark floor, not the lit
    // outside, so a tunnel's deep walls and ceiling keep their occlusion ("only the floor of the tunnel
    // is dark", user 2026-10-11) while an open mushroom underside keeps the exemption.
    ao = lerp(ao, 1.0f, gShadowParams.skyAOGround.x * open * saturate(0.5f - 0.5f * normalize(N).z));
    float3 f = skyAmbFactorAO(N, ao);
    float  g = gShadowParams.skyAOFloor.z;
    if (g <= 0.0f || aoPos >= 1.0f) { return f; }
    float4 m  = gShadowParams.skyAOMap;
    float2 uv = (worldPosRel.xy + gFrameData.lodEye.xy - m.xy) * m.z;
    float2 e  = abs(uv - 0.5f) * 2.0f;
    float  edge = saturate((1.0f - max(e.x, e.y)) * 8.0f);
    float4 fld = SampleLvlTex2D(gGiField, gSamplerBilinearClamp, uv, 0.0f);
    // Weight = "this texel has been written", NOT "how settled it is". The field is PREMULTIPLIED and
    // un-premultiplied below, so rgb/a is the exact radiance from the very first frame after the host
    // clears it (every window scroll). Weighting by alpha itself made each scroll fade the whole bounce
    // out and back in over ~1 s: the "blink" walking a few metres (user, 2026-10-09).
    float  w   = saturate(fld.a * 1.0e4f) * edge;
    if (w <= 0.0f) { return f; }
    // Undo the constant floor skyAmbFactorAO added, then add the field's bounce in its place.
    float ambL = dot(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb, float3(0.299f, 0.587f, 0.114f));
    float fl   = (ambL > 1.0e-6f) ? min(gShadowParams.skyAOFloor.x / ambL, gShadowParams.skyAOFloor.y) : 0.0f;
    float3 amb = max(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb, f3(1.0e-4f));
    float3 bounce = (fld.rgb / max(fld.a, 1.0e-3f)) * g / amb;
    // The GROUND-FACING share under a cover (a giant mushroom's underside) looks at the lit ground
    // around it — exactly the light the field holds — so it takes the bounce by the POSITION's
    // occlusion, not by the exempted ao (which is ~1 there and would hand it none). skyAOGround.z is
    // that share's gain (0 = the old weighting; user, 2026-10-11: "brighten undersides of giant
    // mushrooms"). The floor is removed only where it was added.
    const float under = gShadowParams.skyAOGround.z * max(ao - aoPos, 0.0f);
    return f + (bounce * ((1.0f - ao) + under) - f3(fl) * (1.0f - ao)) * (w * open);
}

// The ambient factor: the PROBE FIELD where it is built (it replaces the sky SH, the sky AO and the GI
// field — what a probe sees already holds all three), the old model elsewhere and as the blend toward
// the window's edge / an unbuilt neighbourhood.
//
// SEALED SPOTS keep the old model. Where a cover spans a whole cell and reaches the ground (a tunnel's
// rock) the column's probes stand on top of it, and a point inside would take the outside's light
// ("tunnel looks dark from outside ... walk inside and it lits up", user 2026-10-11). Such a point is
// BELOW its column's lowest probe (pgiIrradiance's `below`), and there the field's confidence takes
// the old model's OPENNESS (sky visibility, else lateral): a tunnel's deep end is sealed and hands back
// to the old model's dark, its mouth is open and keeps the field. A floating cover (a giant mushroom's
// cap) has its layer 0 on the terrain beneath it (pgiplace), so its underside is above its probe and
// takes the field unconditionally. (Tried first: a low-map "same side of the cover" test, which missed
// the tunnel and dropped the underside; openness everywhere, which also closed the underside; a
// per-texel "under the low map" mode, which drew 64 u blocks where the stems' feet fill the texel.)
float skyOpenness(float ao, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    const float openRef = gShadowParams.skyAOGround.y;
    float open = (openRef > 0.0f) ? smoothstep(0.0f, openRef, ao) : 1.0f;
    const float latR = gShadowParams.skyAOGround.w;
    if (open < 1.0f && latR > 0.0f && gShadowParams.skyAOMap.z > 0.0f) {
        open = max(open, skyLateralOpen(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz, latR));
    }
    return open;
}

// THE FIRST-PERSON MESHES' sky AO: the sky-direction maps' answer AT THE CAMERA (skyvis.comp's eye
// texel), combined with the march exactly as skyAOTermPx combines a screen texel. They are in no
// prepass, so the screen term never holds them, and the march alone hands a ROOFED point back to open
// sky — its overhang correction leaves the roof to GTAO, which the arms do not get either. That lit
// the arms as under open sky in a covered corner ("fp is glowing", user 2026-10-17). The arms span
// under a metre around the eye: one value for all of them is the low-frequency answer.
float skyAOTermEye(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel)
{
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    const float strength = gShadowParams.skyParams.y;
    if (strength > 0.0f && gShadowParams.skyVis.x > 0.0f && gShadowParams.skyVis.w > 0.5f)
    {
        const float4 e = LoadTex2D(gSkyVisEye, NO_SAMPLER, int2(0, 0), 0);
        const float  v = lerp(1.0f, e.x, strength);
        // e.x is already the average over the maps that DO hold the camera, so a partial coverage only
        // says it is near their boxes' edge. Blending by it (as the screen term does) handed most of
        // the answer back to the march, which reads a roofed spot as open: deep in a tunnel the maps
        // said < 0.2 and the arms still lit as under the sky (ibotelmuthadaglow, 10-17). A quarter of
        // the maps is enough to trust it; below that, fade to the march.
        return lerp(skyAOVisibility(worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz), v, saturate(e.z * 4.0f));
    }
#endif
    return skyAOTerm(N, worldPosRel);
}

// ao < 0 = THE FIRST-PERSON ARMS (skyAmbFactorPx): no sky AO of their own, see below.
// bw = the probe volume's band weights (pgiSpecBands); (1,1,1) for every diffuse receiver.
// hiRough >= 0 = a SPECULAR lookup of that roughness: the world probe's cube may refine it (pgiHiSpec).
// Returns the AMBIENT ITSELF (E/pi, ambCol's units), not a factor on ambCol: the probes' light is absolute,
// and as probe / ambCol x ambCol it was multiplied away wherever a channel of MW's ambient is 0 — a cave
// with a black cell ambient took no bounce at all ("the area looks decently lit, but everything else is
// black", iboaddamasartusbounceweak, user 2026-10-23).
float3 skyAmbientAOpW(float3 N, float ao, float3 worldPosRel, float3 bw, float hiRough)
{
    const bool fpAO = ao < 0.0f;
    if (fpAO) { ao = skyAOTermEye(N, worldPosRel); }   // the eye's maps answer: the fallback where no probe answers
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    if (gShadowParams.pgiParams0.x > 0.0f) {
        float below = 0.0f;
        float4 pg;
        // Ball placement: the splatted volume — probes stand where rays reached, and a point a probe
        // cannot see takes nothing from it, so no column / openness gating is needed.
        float pvis = 1.0f;
        float3 plan = f3(0.0f);
        if (gShadowParams.pgiParams2.w > 0.5f) {
            float3 eIrr;   // the same lookup as irradiance (pgiHiSpec's normalization; dropped elsewhere)
            pg = pgiVolIrradianceW(N, worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz, pvis, plan, bw, eIrr);
            // The arms stand in the passage's open middle, where the probes do: they take the probes'
            // sky visibility through their own plane (the gate below then passes the probes' light
            // whole), blended to the march by the volume's confidence. The march cannot see a tunnel's
            // roof, so alone it lit the arms as under open sky ("first person is lit in the tunnels",
            // user 2026-10-17).
            // ...but only while their own AO is the MARCH. With the eye texel (skyAOTermEye) it is the
            // maps' measurement at the camera, which does see a roof: it then goes through the gate below
            // like any receiver's. Overriding it with the probes' visibility passed the volume's light
            // whole to the arms while the walls beside them were gated dark ("fp is glowing", 10-17).
            const bool eyeAO = gShadowParams.skyVis.w > 0.5f && gShadowParams.skyVis.x > 0.0f;
            if (fpAO && !eyeAO && pg.a > 0.0f && gShadowParams.skyParams.y > 0.0f) {
                const float pa = lerp(1.0f, pvis * (0.5f + 0.5f * max(normalize(N).z, 0.0f)), gShadowParams.skyParams.y);
                ao = lerp(ao, pa, saturate(pg.a));
            }
            // RECEIVER VISIBILITY (knob pgiRecvVis, user 2026-10-15: "directional skyAO should normally
            // cover this"): probes stand in the open middle of a passage and see the sky holes a wall
            // or a tunnel floor does not. This point keeps its own sky visibility's share of theirs —
            // as open as the probes: their light as is; a tube's wall under a hole they see: dark.
            // The sky AO is POSITIONAL: a surface loses the dome behind its own plane, 0.5 + 0.5 N.z of
            // it (exact for the cosine-weighted dome), which the probes' directional light already
            // accounts for — so the probes' visibility is taken through that plane. KNEE (giDebug.w):
            // the two estimators disagree by ~0.8 on open ground (receivers among clutter, probes
            // above it); only a ratio under the knee darkens — a tunnel's is ~0.1-0.2.
            const float rv = gShadowParams.giDebug.z;
            // MODE 2 (receiver only): the probes' own visibility drops out. Dividing by it was unstable
            // where the probes see no sky either (deep in a tunnel, Vp ~ 0): the ratio went back to ~1
            // and their bounce passed whole — the root and the bright spots in play (user 2026-10-15).
            // DEBUG (pgiRecvVis 3): the gate's inputs, red = this point's sky AO, green = the probes'
            // sky visibility here (as the share tint does, through ambCol so the albedo x ambient shows it).
            if (rv > 2.5f && rv < 3.5f) { return float3(ao, pvis, 0.0f) * 1.5f; }
            if (rv > 0.0f) {
                const float pv = (rv > 1.5f) ? 1.0f : pvis;
                const float nz = normalize(N).z;
                // A DOWN-facing surface is judged like a wall (plane term floored at 0.5): with the plane
                // at 0 a tunnel's ceiling read r = 1 whatever its sky AO, and kept the probes' light
                // (user 2026-10-15, "last save has a bit of leak"). Its own open question is SIDEWAYS —
                // a giant mushroom's underside sees free air around it, a tunnel's ceiling sees rock —
                // and the probes beneath it measured exactly that: their sky visibility (open all round
                // under a cap, ~5% in a tunnel). The old model's lateral map is off by default (giLatRadius
                // 0: squares, leaks), so the probes answer it.
                float r = saturate((ao + 0.003f) / (pv * (0.5f + 0.5f * max(nz, 0.0f)) + 0.003f));   // a tunnel's probes see ~5% sky: a small epsilon
                // squared (mode 2): a tunnel's leftover visibility stays shut; mode 4 = linear (A/B).
                if (nz < 0.0f) { r = max(r, ((rv > 3.5f) ? pvis : pvis * pvis) * saturate(-2.0f * nz)); }
                const float t  = saturate(r / max(gShadowParams.giDebug.w, 0.01f));                // squared under the knee: open ground (r >= knee) untouched. Only what is NOT the lanterns':
                // their bounce is local light, not sky seen through a hole the receiver lacks. Up to their
                // L0 passes whole (where this normal gets less than the lanterns' mean, all of it is theirs),
                // per channel, so a teal lantern's fill does not carry the far tunnel's warm light in with it.
                const float3 keep = min(pg.rgb, plan);
                pg.rgb = keep + (pg.rgb - keep) * (t * t);
                const float3 keepI = min(eIrr, plan);   // the irradiance through the same gate
                eIrr = keepI + (eIrr - keepI) * (t * t);
            }
            // After the gates: the cube takes the energy the gated volume measured, not the probe's.
            if (hiRough >= 0.0f && pg.a > 0.0f) { pg.rgb = pgiHiSpec(N, hiRough, pg.rgb, eIrr, worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz); }
        }
        else {
            pg = pgiIrradiance(N, worldPosRel + gFrameData.lodEye.xyz, below);
            if (pg.a > 0.0f && below > 0.0f) { pg.a *= lerp(1.0f, skyOpenness(ao, worldPosRel), below); }
        }
        // DEBUG (pgiDebug 2): the ambient becomes a tint of WHO lights this point — red the old model,
        // green the probe field, the blend between them by the field's confidence here.
        if (gShadowParams.pgiParams1.w > 0.5f) {
            const float sh = saturate(pg.a);
            return float3(1.0f - sh, sh, 0.1f) * 1.5f;
        }
        if (pg.a > 0.0f) {
            float3 fp = pg.rgb * gShadowParams.pgiParams0.x;
            // NO FLOOR on the volume (user, 2026-10-14). It was clamped to the old model's constant
            // floor as a stand-in for the lanterns' bounce, but that floor is a fraction of MW's ambCol,
            // which does not follow the sky: at dusk the cap bound at half an ambient ~10x the real sky
            // and lifted the probes 2.6x over the independent truth (iboebontowernearfar). The lanterns
            // belong to point-light GI; the floor stays in the old model only.
            if (pg.a >= 0.999f) { return fp; }
            return lerp(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb * skyAmbFactorAOpOld(N, ao, worldPosRel), fp, pg.a);
        }
    }
#endif
    return gFrameData.ambCol.rgb * skyAmbFactorAOpOld(N, ao, worldPosRel);
}
// ...and as a FACTOR on ambCol, for the receivers whose ambient base is not ambCol (distant statics and
// terrain scale it by lodSunAmb, grass by its vertex colour). Exteriors only, where MW's ambient is never 0.
float3 skyAmbFactorAOpW(float3 N, float ao, float3 worldPosRel, float3 bw, float hiRough)
{
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    if (gShadowParams.pgiParams0.x > 0.0f) {
        return skyAmbientAOpW(N, ao, worldPosRel, bw, hiRough) / max(gFrameData.ambCol.rgb, f3(1.0e-4f));
    }
#endif
    if (ao < 0.0f) { ao = skyAOTermEye(N, worldPosRel); }
    return skyAmbFactorAOpOld(N, ao, worldPosRel);
}
float3 skyAmbFactorAOp(float3 N, float ao, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    return skyAmbFactorAOpW(N, ao, worldPosRel, f3(1.0f), -1.0f);
}

float3 skyAmbFactor(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    return skyAmbFactorAOp(N, skyAOTerm(N, worldPosRel), worldPosRel);
}
// The ambient itself (ambCol x skyAmbFactor where MW's ambient is lit; the probes' light whole where it is 0).
float3 skyAmbient(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel)
{
    return skyAmbientAOpW(N, skyAOTerm(N, worldPosRel), worldPosRel, f3(1.0f), -1.0f);
}

// The MAIN camera's lit fragments: the same factor with the sky-visibility screen term.
// FIRST PERSON (alphaShadowParams.z): the arms are not in the prepass, so the screen term finds no texel
// of theirs and its fallback is the march. They take the probes' visibility instead (ao = -1).
float skyAOTermPxOrArms(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    if (gFrameData.alphaShadowParams.z > 0.5f) { return -1.0f; }
#endif
    return skyAOTermPx(N, worldPosRel, svPos);
}
float3 skyAmbFactorPx(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
    return skyAmbFactorAOp(N, skyAOTermPxOrArms(N, worldPosRel, svPos), worldPosRel);
}
float3 skyAmbientPx(float3 N, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
    return skyAmbientAOpW(N, skyAOTermPxOrArms(N, worldPosRel, svPos), worldPosRel, f3(1.0f), -1.0f);
}

// The SPECULAR environment of a PBR surface: skyAmbFactorPx at the mirror direction R, with the probe
// volume read through this surface's lobe (pgiSpecBands). ONE path, no branch on the strength: at 0 the
// weights are exactly (1,1,1) and x * 1.0 is exact, so it IS skyAmbFactorPx bit for bit — a branch here
// inlined the whole probe lookup twice (+27% opaque.frag) for a difference of nothing.
float3 skyAmbSpecPx(float3 R, float rough, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    const float3 bw = pgiSpecBands(rough, saturate(gShadowParams.pgiSpec.x));
    return skyAmbFactorAOpW(R, skyAOTermPxOrArms(R, worldPosRel, svPos), worldPosRel, bw, rough);
#else
    return skyAmbFactorPx(R, worldPosRel, svPos);
#endif
}
// ...as the ambient itself (skyAmbientPx's units), for the receivers whose base is ambCol.
float3 skyAmbientSpecPx(float3 R, float rough, float3 worldPosRel, float2 svPos)
{
#ifndef PGI_COMPUTE
    const float3 bw = pgiSpecBands(rough, saturate(gShadowParams.pgiSpec.x));
    return skyAmbientAOpW(R, skyAOTermPxOrArms(R, worldPosRel, svPos), worldPosRel, bw, rough);
#else
    return gFrameData.ambCol.rgb * skyAmbFactorPx(R, worldPosRel, svPos);
#endif
}

#endif // SKY_AMB_H
